# Security Best Practices

## Authentication Security

- Always validate authentication server-side
- Never trust client-side auth state alone
- Use HTTP-only cookies for session storage
- Implement proper session timeout
- Use secure cookie settings (Secure, SameSite)
- Rotate tokens regularly
- Implement account lockout after failed attempts
- Log authentication events

## Authorization

- Implement proper authorization checks
- Check permissions on every protected action
- Use Row Level Security (RLS) in Supabase
- Don't rely on UI hiding for security
- Validate permissions server-side
- Use principle of least privilege
- Audit authorization regularly
- Log authorization failures

## Data Protection

- Never expose sensitive data in client code
- Don't log sensitive information
- Mask sensitive data in logs
- Use environment variables for secrets
- Never commit secrets to git
- Encrypt sensitive data at rest
- Use HTTPS everywhere
- Sanitize data before display

## Input Validation

- Validate all user input server-side
- Use Zod schemas for validation
- Sanitize input to prevent XSS
- Validate file uploads (type, size, content)
- Reject unexpected input
- Use allowlists over denylists
- Validate data types and formats
- Check string lengths

## XSS Prevention

- Use React's built-in XSS protection (JSX escaping)
- Avoid dangerouslySetInnerHTML when possible
- Sanitize HTML when necessary (use DOMPurify)
- Set proper Content-Security-Policy headers
- Validate and sanitize user input
- Escape data in different contexts appropriately
- Don't build HTML strings manually
- Use textContent instead of innerHTML

## CSRF Protection

- Use SameSite cookie attribute
- Implement CSRF tokens for state-changing operations
- Verify origin headers
- Use POST for state-changing operations
- Don't use GET for mutations
- Validate referrer headers
- Use double-submit cookie pattern
- Leverage Next.js built-in protections

## SQL Injection

- Use Supabase parameterized queries (automatic)
- Never build SQL queries with string concatenation
- Use Supabase query builder
- Validate input before database operations
- Use type-safe query builders
- Limit database permissions
- Use prepared statements
- Implement query monitoring

## API Security

- Implement rate limiting
- Use authentication for all protected endpoints
- Validate all API inputs
- Return appropriate error codes
- Don't expose internal error details
- Use API versioning
- Implement request throttling
- Log suspicious activity

## Environment Variables

- Use .env.local for local development
- Never commit .env files to git
- Use different values for dev/staging/production
- Prefix public variables with NEXT_PUBLIC_
- Validate required env vars at startup
- Rotate secrets regularly
- Use secret management systems in production
- Document required environment variables

## Dependency Security

- Keep dependencies updated
- Use npm audit or pnpm audit regularly
- Review security advisories
- Use lock files (package-lock.json, pnpm-lock.yaml)
- Minimize dependency count
- Audit new dependencies before adding
- Use automated security scanning
- Monitor for vulnerabilities

## Headers Security

- Set Content-Security-Policy header
- Use X-Frame-Options to prevent clickjacking
- Set X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
- Use Strict-Transport-Security (HSTS)
- Set Referrer-Policy appropriately
- Use Permissions-Policy
- Implement proper CORS headers
- Remove X-Powered-By header

## File Upload Security

- Validate file types (content, not extension)
- Limit file sizes
- Scan files for malware
- Store files outside web root
- Use Supabase Storage with RLS
- Generate unique filenames
- Implement upload rate limiting
- Validate file content

## Password Security

- Use Supabase Auth password hashing
- Enforce strong password requirements
- Implement password strength meter
- Don't store passwords in plain text
- Support password managers
- Implement password reset securely
- Prevent password enumeration
- Consider multi-factor authentication

## Session Management

- Use secure, HTTP-only cookies
- Implement proper session timeout
- Invalidate sessions on logout
- Rotate session tokens
- Use Supabase SSR for session handling
- Implement concurrent session limits
- Log session events
- Handle expired sessions gracefully

## Logging and Monitoring

- Log security events (auth, authorization, errors)
- Monitor for suspicious activity
- Don't log sensitive data
- Implement alerting for security events
- Use structured logging
- Retain logs appropriately
- Implement audit trails
- Review logs regularly

## Supabase Security

- Enable Row Level Security (RLS) on all tables
- Use service_role key only server-side
- Implement proper RLS policies
- Use anon key for client-side
- Enable email verification
- Configure proper OAuth settings
- Use Supabase Edge Functions securely
- Monitor database security

## Error Handling

- Don't expose stack traces to users
- Log errors securely
- Return generic error messages
- Handle errors gracefully
- Don't leak sensitive information in errors
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Monitor error patterns
- Use error tracking services

## Best Practices

- Follow principle of least privilege
- Implement defense in depth
- Keep security up to date
- Conduct security reviews
- Use security linters (ESLint security plugins)
- Implement security testing
- Stay informed about vulnerabilities
- Document security measures
- Train team on security practices
